# width: 120
# The which-key popup is titled with the key that opens it in the ACTIVE
# profile. It used to read `<leader>` in both — but the standard profile
# has no leader to press (`docs/KEYMAP_PROFILES.md` rule 2: the `ctrl+k …`
# menus are its leader), and the Info panel on the same screen already
# says `Ctrl+K Ctrl+I`. A deliberate departure from the reference editor,
# which titles both profiles `<leader>` (`docs/PARITY.md`).
# (Since 2026-09-24 the standard popup IS its `Ctrl+K` chords, from the
# palette and the pause alike — `whichkey_standard_ctrl_k.test`.)
write a.txt "hello\n"
open a.txt
command editor.use_standard
expect screen contains "[F12] Definition"

command whichkey.leader
expect screen lacks "┌ <leader> "
expect screen contains "┌ Ctrl+K "
expect screen contains "g → +git (1)"
# A level inside a group carries the keys typed so far.
type g
expect screen contains "┌ Ctrl+K g "
key esc

# A dead end names the same key rather than a leader that is not there.
command whichkey.leader
type ;
expect screen contains "no Ctrl+K chord: Ctrl+K ;"
expect screen lacks "no leader mapping: <leader>"

# The vim profile keeps Neovim's spelling, header and toast alike.
command editor.use_vim
key space
wait 700
expect screen contains "┌ <leader> "
key esc
